// EXPECT: E0258 — a `switch` on an enum that does not handle every member (ADR-0067 §3)
//
// The point of adding matching is that the compiler can *prove* a case is handled, so a missing member
// is an error rather than a warning — a warning would leave the proof optional, which is the same
// "behaviour depends on something invisible" ADR-0014 §3 refuses.
//
// The message names the members that are missing rather than counting them, because the missing name
// *is* the fix and a count makes the reader re-derive it. Here `BLUE` is unhandled.
//
// Only an enum is checked this way: an `s64` has no finite member set, so a `switch` on one is legal
// without the check and needs an `else` to be total (see `valid/054`'s `classify`).
Colour :: enum {
    RED;
    GREEN;
    BLUE;
}

pick :: (c: Colour) -> s64 {
    r := 0;
    switch c {
        case .RED;
            r = 1;
        case .GREEN;
            r = 2;
    }
    return r;
}
